  ],
  "text": "Before any animal could leave the water, something had to make land survivable. Bare rock is not a place. Nothing eats it and nothing lives on it. Four hundred and seventy million years ago, plants moved onto that rock anyway. Roots split the rock. Dead plants filled the gaps. That mixture is soil. Soil holds water and food. Now something could grow, and something could eat it. And all that photosynthesis was pouring oxygen into the air the whole time. They also pulled out so much carbon that they helped trigger an ice age. The first forests nearly froze the planet, simply by growing on it. Every animal on land is standing on something plants had to build first. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
